# Build output. Nothing that a compiler writes belongs in a repository whose
# whole argument is that binaries should come from a build you can watch.
/target
target/
**/*.rs.bk
/out/
/build/
/artifacts/

# The rebuilt blobs and the reports about them. CI produces these on every run
# and attaches them to a release; a copy committed by hand would be exactly the
# kind of unverifiable file this project exists to remove.
/rebuild/
/reports/
*.stamp

# Toolchain tarballs fetched during a build. They are pinned by hash in
# `toolchains.lock` and downloaded on demand, never vendored.
/toolchains/cache/
*.tar.xz
*.tar.gz
*.tar.bz2
!DOC/*.tar.xz

# Locally generated release-signing material. NEVER commit any of this.
# `tools/signing/generate-signing-key.sh` writes here; the private key and the
# passphrase that protects it live only on the maintainer's machine and in
# GitHub's encrypted secret store. Only the public key and its fingerprint are
# ever meant to leave this folder.
/gpg_secrets/
gpg_secrets/
secring.*
*.key
*.gpg
!*.pub

# Armoured OpenPGP files are ignored by default so that a private key cannot be
# committed by accident. The public key is meant to be published, so the two
# places it legitimately lives are allowed back in explicitly.
*.asc
!docs/keys/ventoy-reproducible-signing-key.asc
!**/public-key.asc

# Working notes and assistant configuration. These are tooling for whoever is
# working on this, not part of what anybody downloads, and a public repository
# is not the place for either.
#
# NOTE, because untracking is not deleting: `git rm --cached` stops a file
# being published from now on, but every version already committed stays in the
# repository's history and can still be fetched by anyone who clones it.
# Removing it from history means rewriting published history and force-pushing,
# which is a separate and deliberate act.
/ai/
ai/
.claude/
.claude*/
CLAUDE.md
**/CLAUDE.md
.mcp.json
.cursor/
.aider*
.codeium/
.github/copilot-instructions.md
HANDOFF.md
HANDOVER.md
NEXT-SESSION.md

# Agent skills. Installed locally for Rust and security review practice; they
# are tooling, not a deliverable, and several carry their own upstream licences.
skills/
**/skills/

# OS and editor noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~
